  <main_text>
    <l n="1" type="heading" level="2" para="true">Tabula de Principijs.</l>
    <l n="2" type="diagram" level="1">Qui de principijs disputarunt, alij posuerunt</l>
    <l n="3" type="diagram" level="2">Unum: sed hoc quidam</l>
    <l n="4" type="diagram" level="3">Immobile: id autè vel</l>
    <l n="5" type="diagram" level="4">Infinitum, sicut Melissus.</l>
    <l n="6" type="diagram" level="4">Finitum, sicut Parmenides.</l>
    <l n="7" type="diagram" level="3">Mobile: sed alij</l>
    <l n="8" type="diagram" level="4">Ignem, ut Heraclitus.</l>
    <l n="9" type="diagram" level="4">Aerem, ut Diogenes.</l>
    <l n="10" type="diagram" level="4">Aquā, ut Thales Milesius.</l>
    <l n="11" type="diagram" level="4">Medium quiddā inter aerē &amp;</l>
    <l n="12" type="diagram" level="4">aquam, siue plenū &amp;</l>
    <l n="13" type="diagram" level="4">vacuum, ut Leusippus.</l>
    <l n="14" type="diagram" level="4">Terrā nemo posuit, propter</l>
    <l n="15" type="diagram" level="4">eius craßitudinem.</l>
    <l n="16" type="diagram" level="2">Plura: que quibusdam placuit esse</l>
    <l n="17" type="diagram" level="3">Finita, caq; ant</l>
    <l n="18" type="diagram" level="4">Duo: ut Plato ponens magnum &amp; parvum, vel</l>
    <l n="19" type="diagram" level="4">ut Anaximander unum &amp; unitati repu-</l>
    <l n="20" type="diagram" level="4">gnans: item Stoici mentem &amp; naturam.</l>
    <l n="21" type="diagram" level="4">Tria. Sic namq; Aristoteles ponit materiam,</l>
    <l n="22" type="diagram" level="4">formam &amp; privationē: ante quem &amp; Plato</l>
    <l n="23" type="diagram" level="4">posuit Deum, materiam &amp; idaam. Hac</l>
    <l n="24" type="diagram" level="4">vero forma est, quam materia induit.</l>
    <l n="25" type="diagram" level="4">Quatuor, quemadmodum Empedocles consti-</l>
    <l n="26" type="diagram" level="4">tuens quatuor elementa.</l>
    <l n="27" type="diagram" level="4">Vel alio numero maiore comprehensa.</l>
    <l n="28" type="diagram" level="3">Infinita: sed quidam voluerunt</l>
    <l n="29" type="diagram" level="4">Genere seu specie esse unum, at</l>
    <l n="30" type="diagram" level="4">figura</l>
    <l n="31" type="diagram" level="4">sive forma diversa, si-</l>
    <l n="32" type="diagram" level="4">cut Democritus ponens infi-</l>
    <l n="33" type="diagram" level="4">nita corpora ἄτομα.</l>
    <l n="34" type="diagram" level="4">Genere diversa seu contraria,</l>
    <l n="35" type="diagram" level="4">sicut Anaxagoras asseruit,</l>
    <l n="36" type="diagram" level="4">omnia confusa in chao quo-</l>
    <l n="37" type="diagram" level="4">dam latuisse.</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The vertical label at the left edge is a brace label and is given as its own line. The mark before "Cæterum" (a small fleuron or hand sign) is not transcribed. The Greek word "ἄτομα" is read from a small, heavily inked scan; the accents may be imperfect. "idaam" is printed as it appears. Long s is normalised to s throughout, and u/v is set by the rule (vel, vero, voluerunt, diversa, Unum); siue and autè keep their printed forms. The page has no catchword other than "Cæterum".</notes>
</page>
